<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mehsana district</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mehsana district is a district of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with its headquarters at Mehsana. It was formed largely from the Kadi division of Baroda State and neighbouring small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, which merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> Province in 1948–1949. It is known for dairy farming and its oil and gas fields.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mehsana-district.jpg" alt="Mehsana district" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krishan 1 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N-GJ-158.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mehsana district, also spelled Mahesana, is one of the 34 districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with its headquarters at the city of Mehsana. It covers over 4,400 square kilometres and has over 2 million people, about three quarters of them living in rural areas. It has 614 villages and ten talukas: Becharaji, Kadi, Kheralu, Mahesana, Vadnagar, Vijapur, Visnagar, Satlasana, Jotana and Unjha. Banaskantha district lies to the north, Patan and Surendranagar to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhinagar">Gandhinagar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> to the south and Sabarkantha to the east. Its main towns include Mehsana, Vijapur, Unjha, Vadnagar, Kalol, Kadi, Visnagar and Kheralu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mehsaji Chavda, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> and an heir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavda-dynasty">Chavda dynasty</a>, established Mehsana. He built the city's Toran, an arc gate, and a temple to the Goddess Toran in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikram-samvat">Vikram Samvat</a> 1414, which is 1358 CE, and a temple dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamunda">Chamunda</a>, according to the authors cited for the story. Another legend says he founded it in Vikram Samvat 1375, 1319 CE. The sources show only inconclusively that the town was founded in the Rajput period.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> conquered Baroda and established Baroda State. They expanded their rule into north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and made Patan their administrative headquarters there, which was later moved to Kadi and, in 1902, to Mehsana. The northern area was divided into eight mahals. The Baroda State railway reached the city on 21 March 1887. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii">Sayajirao Gaekwad III</a> built a palace known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmahal">Rajmahal</a> for his son Fatehsinhrao, which is now a district court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gujarati is the first language of 97.58 per cent of the district's people and Hindi of 1.76 per cent, according to the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, which counted 2,035,064 residents. The district sends members to seven Vidhan Sabha constituencies: Kheralu, Unjha, Visnagar, Bechraji, Kadi, Mahesana and Vijapur. Its higher education includes Ganpat University, Indrashil University and Sankalchand Patel University.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mehsana district” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Katosan was merged into Bombay Province in 1948 and became part of Gujarat in 1960, in present <b>Mehsana district</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/katosan-state">Katosan State</a></li><li><blockquote>Sudasna merged into Bombay Province in 1948 and became part of Gujarat in 1960, in present <b>Mehsana district</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sudasna-state">Sudasna State</a></li></ul></section>
